.TH SUPERV 1 "November 2026" "superv 2.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
superv \- clipboard history and emoji panel with Windows-style shortcuts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B superv
.RI [ option ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B superv
brings the Windows clipboard history (Win+V) and emoji panel (Win+.) to the
Linux desktop. It runs in the background from login; its keyboard shortcuts
run the commands below.
.PP
Only the clipboard history is on at first. The emoji panel, plain-text
paste, colour picker, text from the screen and screenshot tool are each
turned on in the settings (\fBSuper+V\fR, then the gear button), which also
registers their shortcuts with the desktop.
.SH OPTIONS
With no option,
.B superv
opens or closes the clipboard panel (what \fBSuper+V\fR runs).
.TP
.B \-\-show
Open the clipboard panel.
.TP
.B \-\-emoji
Open or close the emoji panel (what \fBSuper+.\fR runs). Add
\fB\-\-tab\fR \fIemoji\fR|\fIkaomoji\fR|\fIsymbols\fR to open it on a tab, or
\fB\-\-search\fR \fItext\fR to open it with a search.
.TP
.BI "\-\-emoji \-\-insert " text
Type \fItext\fR into the focused application.
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
Start in the background (done at login).
.TP
.B \-\-clear
Clear the history; pinned items stay.
.TP
.B \-\-quit
Stop the running instance.
.TP
.B \-\-plain\-paste
Paste the clipboard as plain text (\fBCtrl+Super+Alt+V\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-pick\-color
Pick a colour on the screen (\fBSuper+Shift+C\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-extract\-text
Copy text from an area of the screen (\fBSuper+Shift+T\fR); needs
.BR tesseract (1).
.TP
.B \-\-screenshot
Take a screenshot and mark it up (\fBSuper+Shift+S\fR);
\fB\-\-delay\fR \fIseconds\fR waits first.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Check the setup: background instance, shortcuts, pasting, emoji font,
Tesseract and memory use.
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-hotkeys
Remove all of SuperV's shortcuts from the desktop and give back the ones it
replaced.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/superv/
Settings: \fIconfig.ini\fR, \fIshortcuts.ini\fR, \fIemoji.ini\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/superv/
Clipboard history and pinned items. Unpinned items are removed at the next
boot.
.SH NOTES
Pasting into other applications uses a write-only virtual keyboard
(\fI/dev/uinput\fR), which the package's udev rule lets the user at the
active seat use. SuperV never connects to the network.
.SH SEE ALSO
